## How to work

- Choose the implementation steps, helpers, delegation and tests that fit the task. Keep each
  change easy to inspect.
- Before you cross a checkpoint that a human agreed, follow
  `{{skill}}/references/review.md#agreed-checkpoints`. Checkpoint approval resumes the build. It
  never resumes delivery. A commit or a worker session is not a workflow milestone.
- Before editing a newly discovered area, read the project guidance for it. The notes below
  match the case files and the diff as they were when this brief was read. They do not cover every
  file that you can touch.
- Run the normal project commands through your harness. Choose the repetitions, the regression
  coverage and the baseline comparisons that fit the behavior you change. `wf check` is an optional
  convenience for affected checks. It is not a required certificate.
- Address each applicable recorded correction. Keep its source and scope.
  - When you acted on it: `wf correct --address <id> --note "<action/evidence>"`.
  - When you cannot: `wf correct --block <id> --owner "<recovery owner>" --note "<concrete blocker>"`.
  - Reading this brief is not completion. Acknowledging a message is not completion.
  - Do not edit a person's historical verdict to carry an instruction.
- Record commands, observed results and limits as short evidence rows in the working document.
  Read the earlier findings and the recorded decisions. Repair them in the work and its evidence.
  No response form is prescribed.
- Hand over as `{{wf}}/process/SHOW-ME.md` says: show, don't narrate (ASD-STE100). Do not hand
  over prose alone.
- Use the project setup, reset and diagnostic tools to reach the real scenario. A check that could
  not run because the scenario or state was absent is this build's work. Set it up with the project
  tools. Measure the claim. Restore what you changed. Do not give the finding back.
- If a capability is not available, name what is missing and who owns it. Ordinary work does not
  give you authority to fix every external dependency.
- Ordinary helpers, tests and local corrections are yours. These need a recorded decision and a
  renewed agreement: a new behavior, a consequential contract or persistence choice, a dropped
  agreed item.
- Record each newly found contract path under `paths:` in the ticket's `## Scope`. Then run `wf next`.
- For a real decision or missing authority: write `{{folder}}/BLOCKED.md` with a `Question:` that
  has context. Register it with `wf ask --blocked`. Follow the questions procedure above.
- Prepare the `**T2 walk.**` in the working document. Validate it again against the current
  scenario, also after an earlier build.
  - Record the project's replayable open, setup and readback recipe, or
    `none — <current limitation or why no live walk is needed>`.
  - If a page that was not available can now be shown, update the old `none`.
  - During verification, use the project-owned recipe and the full scenario reset. A helper
    cleanup is not a rollback.
  - Resolve setup and readback gaps before the handoff.
  - Keep useful captures under `{{folder}}/proof/`. Give them clear before and after names.
- For a UI change, before and after PR images are required. Follow
  `{{wf}}/process/SHOW-ME.md#ui-changes-required-pr-images`. Capture the baseline before you edit.
  Put the final pairs under `## UI evidence` in `{{folder}}/{{file}}` before assessment.
